      "content": "The argument that historical figures had \"moral failure\" in action now rather than in the past is both valid and controversial. In the 19th and 20th centuries, many leaders genuinely believed their oppressive regimes were moral imperatives to save their societies from chaos or external enemies, using \"strength\" as the ultimate moral good.\nWhile our modern lens clearly judges their ends as immoral, their own internal logic was often self-consistent, making it a valid historical critique to say they failed to see the moral truth of their time, even if they were never \"wrong\" in their own eyes.",
      "source": "target",
